12. Controlled Inputs

A controlled input is an input whose value is controlled by React state.

Instead of letting the HTML input manage its own value, React stores the value in useState.

import { useState } from "react";

function App() {
    const [name, setName] = useState("");

    return (
        <div>
            <input
                value={name}
                onChange={(e) => setName(e.target.value)}
            />

            <p>Your name: {name}</p>
        </div>
    );
}

The important relationship is:

Input → onChange → setState → state → input value

When the student types something:

onChange runs.
The new value is obtained from e.target.value.
setName() changes the state.
React renders the new value.
Why use controlled inputs?

They make it easy for React to:

Read the current input.
Validate the input.
Clear the input.
Use the input value somewhere else.
Submit the input as part of a form.
