13. Forms

React forms use controlled inputs to collect information from the user.

A form can use onSubmit to handle submission.

function App() {
    const [name, setName] = useState("");

    function handleSubmit(e) {
        e.preventDefault();

        console.log(name);
    }

    return (
        <form onSubmit={handleSubmit}>
            <input
                value={name}
                onChange={(e) => setName(e.target.value)}
            />

            <button type="submit">
                Submit
            </button>
        </form>
    );
}
e.preventDefault()

Normally, submitting an HTML form can cause the browser to reload the page.

In React applications, we usually don't want that.

e.preventDefault();

stops the normal browser submission so React can handle the form.

Multiple inputs

A form can have several pieces of state.

const [name, setName] = useState("");
const [email, setEmail] = useState("");

Then each input controls one state value.

