15. Add / Edit / Delete UI

This is an important step because it starts making React applications feel like real applications.

Suppose we have:

const [items, setItems] = useState([]);

The UI can allow the user to:

Add item
Edit item
Delete item
Add

Use a new object and create a new array.

setItems([
    ...items,
    {
        id: Date.now(),
        name: "Keyboard"
    }
]);

The spread operator keeps the existing items and adds the new item.

Delete

filter() is commonly used.

setItems(
    items.filter(item => item.id !== id)
);

This creates a new array without the selected item.

Edit

Editing usually requires:

Selecting an item.
Putting its information into an input.
Changing the input.
Saving the new information.

For example:

setItems(
    items.map(item =>
        item.id === id
            ? { ...item, name: newName }
            : item
    )
);

The item with the matching ID is replaced with an updated object.

Simple Exercise

Create a small Todo List.

The user should be able to:

