17. Reusable UI Components

As applications become larger, you don't want to write the same UI code repeatedly.

For example, instead of writing three different buttons:

<button>Add</button>
<button>Delete</button>
<button>Edit</button>

you could create a reusable component.

function Button({ children, onClick }) {
    return (
        <button onClick={onClick}>
            {children}
        </button>
    );
}

Then:

<Button onClick={addItem}>
    Add
</Button>

<Button onClick={deleteItem}>
    Delete
</Button>

<Button onClick={editItem}>
    Edit
</Button>

The component handles the common UI structure, while props control what it does.

Reusable Card
function Card({ title, children }) {
    return (
        <div className="card">
            <h2>{title}</h2>
            {children}
        </div>
    );
}

Usage:

<Card title="Profile">
    <p>Name: Alex</p>
</Card>

<Card title="Score">
    <p>Score: 100</p>
</Card>

This combines props and props.children, which the student learned previously.

Good reusable components

A reusable component should have a clear purpose.

Good examples:

Button
Card
Modal
Input
ProductCard
TodoItem
Navbar

The goal isn't to turn every HTML element into a component. The goal is to create components that are useful in multiple places.

